A <diatomic molecule> has $3N-5=1$ vibrational <normal mode>: the bond stretch. For <hydrogen chloride>, opposite longitudinal displacements change the <electric dipole moment>, so the mode has <vibrational infrared activity>. The following original sketches show one phase of this motion and the three water modes; arrow lengths are schematic, not mass-weighted amplitudes.

\Image[paper-1-vibrations.png]
{title=Original sketches of the <hydrogen chloride> stretch and the three <molecular vibrations> of water}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A nonlinear triatomic molecule has $3N-6=3$ vibrational <normal modes>. For water they are the symmetric O–H stretch, the H–O–H bend, and the antisymmetric O–H stretch, shown above. In the symmetric stretch both O–H lengths increase together; in the antisymmetric stretch one increases while the other decreases; the bend predominantly changes the bond angle. All three change a component of the <electric dipole moment> and are infrared-active. Every arrow reverses half a period later; motion of oxygen maintains the molecular <centre of mass>.

= d
{parent=A3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

This is a <rovibrational spectrum>: the vibrational excitation is accompanied by quantized <molecular rotation>. The high-wavenumber R branch has $\Delta J=+1$, and the low-wavenumber P branch has $\Delta J=-1$. In the simplest common-$B$ approximation,
$$
\widetilde\nu_R(J)=\widetilde\nu_0+2B(J+1),\qquad
\widetilde\nu_P(J)=\widetilde\nu_0-2BJ.
$$
Consecutive lines within either branch are therefore separated by roughly $2B$, while the gap between P(1) and R(0) is roughly $4B$. There is no Q branch: for this diatomic parallel $\Sigma\to\Sigma$ transition, the electric-dipole rotational selection rule allows $\Delta J=\pm1$, and the $\Delta J=0$ line strength vanishes.

The intensity envelope comes from rotational populations, approximately $(2J+1)e^{-hcBJ(J+1)/(k_BT)}$, together with the rotational line strengths. The paired peaks arise from the two chlorine <isotopologues>, $\mathrm H^{35}Cl$ and $\mathrm H^{37}Cl$: their different <reduced masses> shift both rotational and vibrational spacings. The more abundant $^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ gives the stronger member. The changing spacing away from the band centre reflects $B_1\ne B_0$ and, at higher $J$, centrifugal distortion; it is not evidence for additional vibrational fundamentals.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the two-body <reduced mass>, rather than the chlorine mass or hydrogen mass alone:
$$
\mu=\frac{1.0078\times34.9688}{1.0078+34.9688}m_u
=1.62661\times10^{-27}\ \mathrm{kg}.
$$
The <bond force constant> gives
$$
\nu_{\rm harm}=\frac1{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{481}{\mu}}=8.65467\times10^{13}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad
\boxed{\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}=\frac{\nu_{\rm harm}}{2.99792458\times10^{10}\ \mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}}=2886.89\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
The requested “frequency in cm−1” is a <spectroscopic wavenumber>; the ordinary frequency is the value in s−1 above. This calculation uses the question's force constant in the harmonic model, rather than replacing it with a tabulated spectroscopic constant.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Read the stronger members of the inset doublets at about $2945$, $2926$ and $2906\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. Their spacings are roughly $19$–$20\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, so the common-$B$ estimate is
$$
\boxed{B\simeq\tfrac12\Delta\widetilde\nu\simeq10\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
Using the harmonic wavenumber from the preceding calculation and labelling lines by the lower-state $J$,
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\nu_{P_1}\simeq2886.89-2(10)=2867\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}},\qquad
\widetilde\nu_{R_1}\simeq2886.89+4(10)=2927\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
These are graph-based estimates. Finite reading precision and different upper/lower <rotational constants> limit their accuracy to a few cm−1; extra decimal places would not be justified. The R1 result refers to $J=1\to2$, while R0 is the line nearest the high-wavenumber side of the band gap.

= A4
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $Q_k$ be a vibrational <normal mode coordinate> with symmetry $\Gamma_k$. The harmonic vibrational ground state is totally symmetric; the one-quantum state transforms as $Q_k$. Integration over a symmetry-invariant domain annihilates any integrand lacking the <totally symmetric representation>. Thus a fundamental requires
$$
\Gamma(v'=1_k)\otimes\Gamma(\mu_\alpha)\otimes\Gamma(v''=0)
\supset A_1
$$
for some Cartesian component $\alpha$. For the one-dimensional irreducible representations used here, this means $\Gamma_k=\Gamma(x)$, $\Gamma(y)$ or $\Gamma(z)$. Equivalently, expanding the <electric dipole moment> gives
$$
\mu_\alpha=\mu_{\alpha,0}+\left(\frac{\partial\mu_\alpha}{\partial Q_k}\right)_0Q_k+\cdots;
$$
the derivative can be nonzero only if $Q_k$ and $\mu_\alpha$ have compatible symmetry. This is the symmetry criterion for <vibrational infrared activity>; it permits a transition without guaranteeing a large intensity.

