= Solution
{parent=i}

With $z$ along C=O and the molecule in the $xz$ plane, <formaldehyde> has $E$, $C_2(z)$, $\sigma_{xz}$ and $\sigma_{yz}$. It belongs to the <C2v point group>, $\boxed{C_{2v}}$. Keeping this axis convention is essential when assigning the labels $B_1$ and $B_2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For operations ordered as $(E,C_2(z),\sigma_{xz},\sigma_{yz})$, the characters of the twelve Cartesian displacements are
$$
\chi_{3N}=(12,-2,4,2).
$$
Only atoms left fixed contribute: all four for $E$ and $\sigma_{xz}$, but only C and O for $C_2$ and $\sigma_{yz}$. Multiplying their counts by the Cartesian traces $3,-1,1,1$ gives these numbers. Subtract
$$
\chi_{\rm trans}=(3,-1,1,1),\qquad \chi_{\rm rot}=(3,-1,-1,-1)
$$
to obtain $\chi_{\rm vib}=(6,0,4,2)$. The <character table> reduction gives
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{\rm vib}=3A_1+2B_1+B_2.}
$$
The dimensions sum to $6=3N-6$, as required. The $B_2$ mode is the out-of-plane wag with this axis convention.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In this <C2v point group> convention, $z\sim A_1$, $x\sim B_1$, and $y\sim B_2$. Therefore all six <normal modes> have symmetry-allowed infrared fundamentals: $\boxed{6\text{ IR features}}$, absent accidental coincidences or vanishing intensities.

For <Raman spectroscopy>, examine the <polarizability tensor>: $x^2,y^2,z^2\sim A_1$, $xz\sim B_1$, and $yz\sim B_2$; $xy\sim A_2$ is also Raman-allowed but no $A_2$ vibration occurs here. All six vibrations are therefore Raman-active too, giving $\boxed{6\text{ Raman features and }6\text{ IR/Raman coincidences}}$. The centrosymmetric mutual-exclusion rule does not apply to $C_{2v}$ <formaldehyde>.

= c
{parent=A4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The C=O length is invariant under every operation, so its stretch is $A_1$. If $s_L,s_R$ are the two C–H bond-length changes, their sum and difference give
$$
Q_{\rm CO}\sim A_1,\qquad Q_{\rm CH,s}=\frac{s_L+s_R}{\sqrt2}\sim A_1,\qquad
Q_{\rm CH,a}=\frac{s_L-s_R}{\sqrt2}\sim B_1.
$$
Hence $\boxed{\Gamma_{\rm stretch}=2A_1+B_1}$. These are stretching symmetry coordinates; the two $A_1$ motions can mix in actual <normal modes>, and mass-weighted atomic motions need not have the schematic arrow lengths below.

\Image[paper-1-formaldehyde-stretches.png]
{title=Original <formaldehyde> stretching symmetry sketches in the paper's $xz$ plane}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

There are three stretching fundamentals, although four stretching-region features are listed. The low feature at $1746\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is predominantly C=O stretch ($A_1$). The two features at $2783$ and $2843\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ are the symmetric and antisymmetric C–H stretches; their order is not determined by this mode count alone.

The $3482\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ feature is consistent with the first C=O <vibrational overtone>, close to $2(1746)=3492\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and shifted downward by <anharmonicity>. Its symmetry is $A_1\otimes A_1=A_1$, so symmetry permits the weak anharmonic transition. It is not a fourth stretching fundamental. The combination-band evidence in the next part distinguishes the two C–H assignments.

= d
{parent=A4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The out-of-plane displacements in the wag transform as $y$, hence as $B_2$. A one-quantum combination of two distinct <normal modes> transforms as the product of their irreducible representations. The observed feature satisfies
$$
3950-1167=2783\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.
$$
A combination of the wag with an $A_1$ stretch has symmetry $B_2\otimes A_1=B_2$ and can absorb through $\mu_y$. In contrast, $B_2\otimes B_1=A_2$ has no electric-dipole component in $C_{2v}$ and is forbidden in the stated symmetry model. The missing feature near $4010=1167+2843\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ therefore identifies the $2843$ stretch as $B_1$.

The completed assignments are
$$
\boxed{1746:\ \mathrm{C{=}O}\ (A_1),\quad2783:\ \mathrm{CH\ symmetric}\ (A_1),\quad2843:\ \mathrm{CH\ antisymmetric}\ (B_1),\quad3482:\ 2\nu_{\rm CO}\ (A_1).}
$$
The allowed combination obtains intensity through anharmonic/dipole-coupling terms; a purely separable harmonic model with a strictly linear dipole has no combination bands. The argument uses both the observed combination and the selection rule: absence of a feature by itself would not generally prove a forbidden symmetry.<Formaldehyde vibrational data in the NIST Chemistry WebBook>[Gas-phase fundamentals: CH2 symmetric stretch 2783, antisymmetric stretch 2843, CO stretch 1746, wag 1167 cm−1, with the same symmetry convention]{ref}

